---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "connect",
 "name": "Netlify",
 "title": "How to Connect a Domain to Netlify",
 "description": "Point your custom domain to Netlify: exact records, method choice, and verification.",
 "intro": "Connecting an existing domain to Netlify takes two DNS changes done carefully. Here is the exact method and the verification step.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Add the custom domain",
   "Netlify, Site configuration, Domain management: Add custom domain."
  ],
  [
   "Point DNS",
   "Either Netlify DNS (nameservers) or external DNS: A record at @ to Netlify's load-balancer IP (75.2.60.5) and CNAME www to your netlify.app subdomain, as displayed."
  ],
  [
   "HTTPS",
   "Netlify provisions Let's Encrypt certificates automatically once DNS verifies."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "Netlify's recommended external setup uses the load-balancer A record (single stable IP) rather than many rotating IPs; follow the values in your site's domain panel.",
 "faqs": [
  [
   "Apex on Netlify without ALIAS?",
   "The load-balancer A record makes the apex work with plain A records; no flattening tricks needed."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in Netlify?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find Netlify's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for {name} to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in Netlify?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in Netlify?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in {name}, and keep those records unproxied."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
